   Ergonomics and Human Factors, Social and Occupational Ergonomics, and
   Cross-Cultural Decision Making, ELECTR NETWORK, JUL 25-29, 2021},
Abstract = {This study aims to examine how human service employees' positive and
   negative emotions affect their task performance. Affective delivery,
   presenting positive emotions in the service interaction, is used as a
   positive emotion, and emotional exhaustion is an employees' stressful
   negative emotion. The author tested hypotheses by using a multiple
   mediation model. The variables used in this study are first measured
   using descriptive statistics and correlation analysis. The author
   verified direct and mediator effects between the variables used in this
   study by path analysis of covariance-based structural equation modeling
   (SEM). The result shows that employees who practice positive emotional
   expression in service interaction can improve job performance and
   increase job satisfaction. Understanding this mechanism could also help
   prevent stress and emotional exhaustion among employees in the
   workplace.},
