Note = {International Conference on Green Buildings and Sustainable Cities
   (GBSC), Bologna, ITALY, SEP 15-16, 2011},
Abstract = {With urban development and economic growth, the conflicts between urban
   development and land shortage are becoming increasingly striking. An
   urban development featuring land shortage due to high population and
   urban expansion are leading to endangered urban water environment; human
   beings' asking for land from water bodies inevitably causes serious
   damage to urban lakes. Wuhan, a big city located in central China,
   abounds in lakes, rivers and water. Specifically, there are 164 lakes in
   Wuhan's main urban area(WMUA). Despite of its advantageous geographical
   environment, a growing number of lakes in Wuhan are filled and
   encroached as a product of the swelling desire of human beings for land.
   This study takes WMUA's major 27 lakes as its research object, selects
   1989, 2000 and 2009 as its timeframes, and investigates the
   characteristics of the changes of the 27 lakes via Geographic
   Information System(GIS) analysis. It also characterizes the changes of
   the lakes from 1989 to 2000 and from 2000 to 2009 via classification.
