Note = {4th International Conference on Emotions and Organizational Life,
   Birkbeck Coll, London, ENGLAND, JUN, 2004},
Abstract = {Emotion work can be defined as demands to display organizationally
   desired emotions regarding service-worker-customer interactions, as well
   as the psychological strategies necessary to regulate these emotional
   demands. This study applies a task-focused concept of emotion work and
   uses the Frankfurt Emotion Work Scales (FEWS) in a cross-cultural
   context to measure emotional work demands. The original German FEWS was
   translated into English and the extent to which the new English FEWS is
   equivalent to the original German FEWS is evaluated. Cultural effects on
   emotion work job demands are demonstrated by comparisons between a US (N
   = 51) and German (N = 202) travel agent sample. Cultural comparisons
   suggest that emotional demands in the US sales service include less
   emotional dissonance (i.e. the requirement to show emotions not actually
   felt in a situation) than in Germany. Survey results are discussed in
   terms of implications for further cross-cultural research.},
