Organization = {Urban Studies Fdn},
Abstract = {A growing consensus suggests that urban scholarship can benefit from
   engaging with knowledge in multiple sites in and beyond the academy, for
   instance with activism central to politics in cities worldwide. This
   paper explores narratives of urban politics produced with activists to
   reflect on how we account for knowledge and practice in theorising the
   urban as political terrain. Research conducted with activists in a
   community-based Civic' organisation in Cape Town, South Africa
   highlights the everyday toil that shapes resistance and its politics,
   the frictions and engagements behind protest headlines. In opening up
   theorisations of activism and revolution to the knowledge practices of
   movements, the paper argues we may more cogently take account of the
   strategic and intimate politics that characterise contemporary activism,
   and reshape notions of revolution', urban politics and research practice
   in contemporary cities, a project broadly relevant in the Global South
   and North.},
