   Groundwater Quality, BIRMINGHAM, ENGLAND, JUL 18-30, 1999},
Organization = {Int Assoc Hydrol Sci, Int Commiss Surface Water; Int Assoc Hydrol Sci,
   Int Commiss Ground Water; Int Assoc Hydrol Sci, Int Commiss Water Qual;
   Int Assoc Hydrol Sci, Int Commiss Continental Eros; Int Union Geodesy \&
   Geophys; Int Assoc Water Qual; Int Assoc Hydrgeol},
Abstract = {Greater Cairo is faced with increases in population and unplanned
   extensions in urbanization. This has not been followed by an equal
   expansion in water supply and sanitary drainage, thus resulting in poor
   environmental conditions. Cairo covers 300 km(2) with intensive and
   varied urban land uses and encompasses the oldest industrial areas of
   Egypt, such as Shubra-Mostorad and Helwan. In addition, new industrial
   communities are located in the western desert fringe of Greater Cairo
   with many industrial areas located in zones of highly vulnerable
   groundwater. Pollution sources of industrial origin threatening
   groundwater occur in a wide variety of both large and small industrial
   areas. Moreover the desert fringes of Cairo are being reclaimed by using
   sewage (waste) water for irrigation. The adverse environmental impacts
   of unplanned urban expansion and industrial and agricultural activities
   could lead to deterioration in groundwater quality. This has dictated
   quick interventions based on groundwater quality monitoring systems
   designed to detect pollution sources and their spatial extension. This
   paper presents groundwater quality indicators for domestic, agricultural
   and industrial pollution at representative sites within Greater Cairo.},
