Note = {7th International Conference Early Childhood Care and Education (ECCE),
   Moscow, RUSSIA, MAY 17-20, 2018},
Abstract = {The article describes modern types and risks of cyber vandalism in
   teenagers' environment. Vandal activity in social networks is steadily
   increasing (Mursalieva, 2017). New types of cyber vandalism appear in
   teenager's environment of megalopolises, where involvement in social
   networks tends to 100\%. Teenagers don't see anything reprehensible
   committing vandalism in the network. Hacking other's pages, Internet
   trolling and cyber bullying do not seem to be the big evil and become
   ordinary. However, the alternative consequences for the victims and the
   actors themselves present serious risks for the socialization and mental
   and physical health of the underage. Teenagers' vandalism is also active
   in the virtual space; city-dwellers spit out dissatisfaction and search
   for profits in networks, hacking someone else's accounts and creating
   fake ones.
   Urban environment requires city-dwellers to increase the pace of social
   transactions, and teenagers' psyche doesn't stand up, so teenagers are
   increasingly trying to find a way out of accumulated aggression in
   social networks. Starting trolling classmates or in search of shelter
   from the daily urban race, teenagers become victims of cyber bullying
   spending time in the networks. Unsafe behaviour in the networks leads to
   loss of confidentiality, because interviewed respondents do not consider
   hacking someone else's data as something reprehensible. Empirical data
   is gathered in Ekaterinburg in teens' chats, opened and closed on-line
   groups of VK.com, as well as through questioning, processed by
   statistical packages and analyzed in a cross-disciplinary manner within
   psychology, sociology, philosophy, economic and social development of
   the territory. (C) 2018 Published by Future Academy
