Note = {4th International Conference on Urban Air Quality, Charles Univ, Prague,
   CZECH REPUBLIC, MAR 25-28, 2003},
Abstract = {Despite the progress made in controlling local air pollution, urban
   areas show increasing signs of environmental stress and air quality is
   one of the major concerns. The findings of several studies provide
   evidence that the shape of a city and the land use distribution
   determine the location of emission sources and the pattern of urban
   traffic, affecting urban air quality.
   This work focuses on the potential impacts of different land use
   patterns oil urban air quality. Three imaginary cities were created,
   based on alternative urban planning strategies, considering different
   land use patterns: from the scenario of urban sprawl to the opposite
   scenario of a compact city with mixed land use. The mesoscale
   photochemical system MEMO/MARS was applied to evaluate the urban air
   quality in each of the idealized city structures.
   Simulation results indicate that more compact cities with mixed land use
   provide better urban air quality compared to disperse and network
   cities. (c) 2005 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.},
